--TEST--
A request no mount stripped a prefix from reads its whole path, the prefix `""` and no captures, so the prefix and the path join back into the path that arrived -- `rule:http-server/a-request-resolves-in-five-steps`, `rule:http-server/a-mount-table-expands-at-boot` and `rule:routing/a-request-reads-its-mount`
--HEADERS--
Host: example.test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: tools:server/mounts-which-file-answers-a-request
// Step 2 strips the matched prefix, and the mount at `/` is the one prefix
// that comes off nothing. It reads back as `""` and never as `/`: a program
// builds a link as the prefix followed by a path, and a `/` there would put
// `//` in front of every link a deployment with no mount block builds.
Core\Request\Mount $mount = Core\Request::mount();
tainted string $path = Core\Request::path();
echo "path: ", $path, "\n";
echo "prefix: [", $mount->prefix(), "]\n";
echo "captures: ", Core\Arr::count($mount->captures()), "\n";
echo "joined: ", $mount->prefix() . $path, "\n";
--EXPECT--
path: /
prefix: []
captures: 0
joined: /
